1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,600
This car is speeding up.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Now watch the wheel.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,200
It seems to turn backwards!

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,200
Now it seems to stand still.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,600
The car never slowed down.
So what is going on?

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,900
Engineering Phenomena, episode 02: Aliasing.
Why wheels spin backwards on video.

7
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:28,800
A video is not smooth motion.

8
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,600
It is a flip book:
30 still pictures every second.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,200
Between two pictures,
the camera sees nothing at all.

10
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,150
Your brain joins the pictures together...

11
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,850
...by linking each spoke to the
closest spoke in the next picture.

12
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:53,000
Let's slow it right down,
and paint one spoke yellow.

13
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:58,200
A small turn between pictures:
it looks like it turns forwards. Correct!

14
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:03,400
Now it turns almost one
spoke gap between pictures.

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,400
Every spoke lands just short
of where the next one was...

16
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:14,600
...so the closest match is backwards.
The wheel seems to turn backwards!

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Turn exactly one gap per picture,
and every picture looks the same.

18
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,800
The wheel seems frozen,
even though it is spinning fast.

19
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:32,400
Engineers call this aliasing: a fast motion
that shows up under a false name, an alias.

20
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:38,800
The camera takes 30 pictures per second.
The spokes pass by f times per second.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:46,600
Up to 15 per second, the video tells the truth.
Faster than that, the motion is disguised.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,400
At exactly 30 or 60 per second the wheel
looks frozen. Just below, it looks backwards.

23
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:00,800
The rule: take samples more than twice as
fast as the fastest change you want to see.

24
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,900
Engineers call it the
Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem.

25
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,600
Helicopter blades can
look frozen on video.

26
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,800
Between two pictures, each blade moves
exactly into the next blade's place.

27
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:22,200
Flickering lights can play
the same trick on machines.

28
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:27,900
A spinning lathe can look stopped. That
is why workshop lights must not flicker.

29
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:33,800
Machines sample the world too: their
sensors are read many times per second.

30
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:39,600
A 900 Hz vibration read 1000 times per
second looks like a slow 100 Hz wave.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,800
So engineers filter out what is too fast,
then sample more than twice as fast.

32
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,900
And a strobe light uses aliasing on purpose,
to freeze fast machines for inspection.

33
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:59,200
Take pictures too slowly, and fast things
can look slow, stopped, or even backwards.

